MARKOV CHAIN
→ 519.2  
→ Probability Theory / Stochastic Processes

A discrete-time stochastic process {X_t} on a finite state space S = {S1, S2, S3}
satisfying the Markov property:

  P(X_{t+1} = j | X_t = i, X_{t-1}, ..., X_0) = P(X_{t+1} = j | X_t = i) = p_ij

The future depends only on the present state, not on the path that led there.


STATES AND TRANSITIONS (example from diagram)
---------------------------------------------

                    p11
                     ↺
                   ( S1 )
                  /     \
             p12 /       \ p13
                /         \
         p22 ↺ ( S2 ) ←→ ( S3 ) ↺ p33
                    p23   p32

  S1 : start / transient-looking state (no incoming arrows from S2 or S3 in this graph)
  S2 : green state
  S3 : red state

  p_ij = P(go to Sj | currently in Si)

  In this particular graph:
    p21 = 0
    p31 = 0
  (once you leave S1 you cannot return)


TRANSITION MATRIX P  (row-stochastic)
-------------------------------------

        to S1    to S2    to S3
from S1 [ p11     p12      p13  ]
from S2 [  0      p22      p23  ]
from S3 [  0      p32      p33  ]

Every row sums to 1:

  p11 + p12 + p13 = 1
  p22 + p23       = 1
  p32 + p33       = 1


KEY TYPES / OBJECTS
-------------------
  State space          S = {1,2,3}  (or {S1,S2,S3})
  Transition matrix    P ∈ ℝ^{3×3}   row-stochastic
  n-step matrix        P^n
  Distribution         π_t  (row vector of probabilities at time t)
  Evolution            π_{t+1} = π_t P
  Stationary dist.     π = π P     (if the chain is irreducible + aperiodic)


FUNDAMENTALS
------------
  • Memoryless (Markov property)
  • P is a stochastic matrix
  • Multi-step probabilities live in matrix powers: (P^n)_{ij}
  • Under irreducibility + aperiodicity, P^n → matrix whose every row is the
    unique stationary distribution π
  • PageRank, simple language models, weather models, and many MCMC methods
    are all instances of this structure
